.TH VSOUNDD 8 "2026-10-05" "VLHE 1.0.1" "VLHE Manual"
.SH NAME
vsoundd \- carry VLHE's mixed sound to the real sound card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vsoundd
.RB [ \-d
.IR card ]
.RB [ \-v
.IR vsound ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-S
.IR rate ]
.RB [ \-R ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B vsound.o
kernel module gives every program its own
.I /dev/dsp
and mixes what they play.
.B vsoundd
is the pump: it reads the mixed stream from vsound and writes it to
the real sound card, whose driver usually allows only one user. The card is
held by
.B vsoundd
alone, so any number of programs can play through it at once.
.PP
It mixes in 16-bit stereo. It opens the card and asks it for 44100 Hz,
or the rate given with
.BR \-S ;
whatever rate the card grants is the rate vsound then mixes at, and
every program's audio is resampled to it. If the card grants
something other than was asked,
.B vsoundd
says so.
.PP
If the card is busy - another program has it open - it waits up to
10 seconds for it before giving up. It refuses to use vsound's own
device as the card, since the pump would then feed itself.
.PP
It is normally started by
.B vlhe apply
or the control centre's Load button, with options taken from
.IR /etc/vlhe.conf ,
and runs until it is sent SIGTERM or SIGINT. Only one may run at a
time: a second refuses to start.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " card"
The real sound card's device. Default
.IR /dev/dsp1 .
When VLHE starts it, this is
.B [Sound Settings] CardDevice
or, when that is empty, the first sound card found that is not
vsound.
.TP
.BI \-v " vsound"
vsound's device, which
.B vsoundd
reads the mix from. Default
.IR /dev/dsp .
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Also write everything to
.IR file ,
as raw 2-channel, 16-bit little-endian samples at the mix rate -
44100 Hz unless the card or
.B \-S
made it something else. If the card
cannot be opened, the capture still runs, so the mixer's output can
be checked when the card is the suspect.
.TP
.BI \-S " rate"
Ask the card for
.I rate
Hz instead of 44100. The card still decides: a rate it cannot do is
clamped by its driver, and the clamped rate is used. vsound mixes at
4000 to 48000 Hz only, so a
.I rate
outside that is refused. If a card grants a rate outside it on its
own,
.B vsoundd
says so: the mix then stays at its old rate and sound plays at the
wrong speed. Useful for a card whose driver reports rates it
does not really run at - Corel's packaged emu10k1 driver echoes back
any rate it is asked for; that chip runs at 48000. Passed from
.B [Sound Settings] MixRate
when that is between 8000 and 48000.
.TP
.B \-R
Release the card when the last program stops playing, so the card's
driver runs its own teardown, and take it again when a program
starts. Off by default: on a card that does not need it, it only adds
the risk of failing to take the card back. Turn it on if you hear a
tone when nothing is playing. The control centre's "Release the card
when the last program stops" sets it.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: leave out the start-up report. Problems, and the running
counts of writes it prints every so often, still appear.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/run/vlhe/ctl/vsoundd.pid
The process ID, so the control centre and
.B vlhe status
can find it.
.TP
.I /var/run/vlhe/ctl/vsoundd.state
Whether it is waiting for a busy card or ready. The CD and MIDI
daemons read it to know when to send their sound to vsound. Removed
when it stops.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped by a signal after running normally, or stopped because
reading from vsound or writing to the card failed, or because another
pump was already reading vsound.
.TP
.B 1
A bad option; another
.B vsoundd
is already running; vsound's device could not be opened; the card
could not be opened and
.B \-o
was not given; or
.B \-R
found another pump already holding vsound.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run by hand against the second card, capturing the mix:
.PP
.RS
vsoundd \-d /dev/dsp2 \-o /tmp/mix.raw
.RE
.SH BUGS
The card cannot yet be changed while
.B vsoundd
runs; a change to Play through takes effect at the next load.
